+Table~\ref{tab:path232-safe-edges} prints actual edge endpoints.
+Target columns use the following conventions.
+
+For an end-change target, map $(0,1,2,3)$ to $(a,b,h_0,h_1)$,
+where $a$ is repeated, $b$ is the singleton and $h_0<h_1$ are unused;
+reverse the path when the singleton is at the left end.
+For a middle-change target $(a,b,a)$, use this colour map; columns
+$0,1$ mean without and with path reversal. The two unused-colour
+orders give the same edge set.
+For an all-distinct target $(a,b,c)$ with unused colour $d$, use
+$(a,b,c,d)$. The other orientation is the map $(c,b,a,d)$ followed
+by path reversal, and gives the same edge set.
+
+The unused-colour interchange $(2\ 3)$ preserves $D_E,D_M$
+and their special edges. Path reversal followed by $(0\ 2)$
+preserves $D_A$. The corresponding target orbits are
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|l}
+001&\{002,003\},\ \{011\},\ \{021,031\},\ \{101\},\ \{201,301\}\\
+010&\{012,013\},\ \{020,030\},\ \{210,310\}\\
+012&\{013,312\},\ \{032\}.
+\end{array}
+\]
+The target families are closed under these actions. Only a
+middle-change target has two distinct cycle edge sets, giving
+twelve entries in the ten representative rows below. Every printed
+edge has an unchanged middle coordinate and is outside both special
+edge sets; the table therefore proves more than mere state overlap.
